 {c:"Behavioral",d:2,q:"Describe a time you went above and beyond.",k:["Show you saw a gap nobody asked you to fix","Explain the extra effort and why you chose it","Quantify the impact on the team or result","Avoid sounding like you want credit"]},
 {c:"Behavioral",d:2,q:"Tell me about a time you persuaded someone to change their mind.",k:["Start with their view and why it made sense to them","Use data or a clear argument, not pressure","Show how you respected the relationship","End with the outcome"]},
 {c:"Behavioral",d:3,q:"You made a mistake that had real consequences. Tell me about it.",k:["Own the mistake early without excuses","Explain how you contained the damage and told people right away","Show the fix and the process change that followed","Choose a real mistake, not a disguised strength"]},
 {c:"Behavioral",d:3,q:"You disagree with your VP on how to build a model. What do you do?",k:["Check first that you really understand their approach","Raise your concern privately and with evidence","Offer to show both versions if time allows","In the end, follow their call and make it work"]},
 {c:"Behavioral",d:3,q:"You're assigned a task you think is pointless. How do you react?",k:["Do it well anyway, and ask for the context behind it","Assume there may be a reason you can't see","Suggest an improvement politely after delivering","Shows attitude: analysts do plenty of unglamorous work"]},
 {c:"Behavioral",d:3,q:"Why should we believe you can handle 100-hour weeks?",k:["Point to proof: demanding seasons, sports, startup or exam crunches","Be honest about the cost and why it's worth it to you","Mention routines that keep you sharp","Avoid sounding glib about it"]},
 {c:"Accounting",d:1,q:"What is EBITDA and why do bankers use it?",k:["Earnings before interest, taxes, depreciation and amortization","Rough proxy for operating cash flow","Ignores capital structure and accounting choices, so easier to compare companies","Limits: ignores capex, working capital and taxes"]},
 {c:"Accounting",d:1,q:"What's the difference between cash and accrual accounting?",k:["Accrual records revenue when earned and expenses when incurred","Cash records them when money moves","Public companies use accrual under GAAP/IFRS","That's why net income and cash flow differ"]},
 {c:"Accounting",d:1,q:"What is depreciation, and why does it exist?",k:["Spreads the cost of a long-lived asset over its useful life","Matches expense to the periods the asset helps earn revenue","Non-cash expense, so it's added back on the cash flow statement","Lowers taxable income, which saves real cash"]},
 {c:"Accounting",d:2,q:"What's the difference between capex and operating expenses?",k:["Capex buys long-lived assets and is capitalized, then depreciated","Opex is expensed immediately on the income statement","Capex hits the cash flow statement under investing","Classification can change reported earnings a lot"]},
 {c:"Accounting",d:2,q:"Accounts receivable goes up by $20. How does the cash flow statement change?",k:["Revenue was booked but cash wasn't collected","Increase in AR is a use of cash: subtract $20 in operating cash flow","Net income is unchanged","Same logic applies to inventory increases"]},
 {c:"Accounting",d:2,q:"What's the difference between LIFO and FIFO, and why does it matter?",k:["FIFO assumes the oldest inventory is sold first, LIFO the newest","With rising prices LIFO gives higher COGS and lower taxable income","FIFO shows higher profits and higher inventory value","LIFO is allowed under US GAAP but not IFRS"]},
 {c:"Accounting",d:2,q:"A company issues $100 of debt. Walk me through the three statements.",k:["Income statement: no change right away","Balance sheet: cash up $100, debt up $100","Cash flow statement: financing activities up $100","Interest expense shows up in later periods"]},
 {c:"Accounting",d:2,q:"Inventory is written down by $10 with a 40% tax rate. Walk me through the statements.",k:["Income statement: pre-tax income down $10, net income down $6","Cash flow statement: start with net income down $6, add back $10 non-cash, so cash is up $4","Balance sheet: inventory down $10, cash up $4, retained earnings down $6","The $4 is the tax saving"]},
 {c:"Accounting",d:2,q:"What is a deferred tax liability and how can one arise?",k:["Taxes owed in the future but not yet paid","Arises when tax expense on the income statement exceeds cash taxes","Example: accelerated depreciation for tax, straight-line for books","Shows up as a liability on the balance sheet"]},
 {c:"Accounting",d:2,q:"A company pays $100 of dividends. Walk me through the statements.",k:["Income statement: no change, dividends aren't an expense","Balance sheet: cash down $100, retained earnings down $100","Cash flow statement: financing activities down $100","Net income is unaffected"]},
 {c:"Accounting",d:3,q:"A company does a $100 share buyback. Walk me through the statements.",k:["Income statement: no direct change","Balance sheet: cash down $100, equity down $100 through treasury stock","Cash flow statement: financing down $100","Fewer shares outstanding, so EPS rises"]},
 {c:"Accounting",d:3,q:"A company changes from straight-line to accelerated depreciation. What changes?",k:["Higher depreciation in early years, lower later","Net income is lower early on, higher later","If taxes follow, early cash taxes fall and cash is higher early","Total depreciation over the asset's life is unchanged"]},
 {c:"Accounting",d:3,q:"Why do some analysts object to adding back stock-based compensation?",k:["It's non-cash, so it's added back in operating cash flow","But it's a real cost: employees are paid in shares that dilute owners","Adding it back overstates profitability, especially at tech companies","Many bankers treat it as an expense or count the dilution"]},
 {c:"Enterprise Value",d:1,q:"Why do we compare companies on enterprise value rather than equity value?",k:["EV captures the whole business, not just shareholders","Companies with different debt levels become comparable","EV is capital-structure neutral","Equity value alone ignores what lenders are owed"]},
 {c:"Enterprise Value",d:1,q:"What is market capitalization?",k:["Share price times shares outstanding","The market's value of a company's equity","Also called equity value","Use diluted shares for a more accurate number"]},
 {c:"Enterprise Value",d:2,q:"Should you use basic or diluted shares when calculating equity value?",k:["Diluted, because options and convertibles can become shares","Use the treasury stock method for options","Only in-the-money options and convertibles count","Basic shares understate value of equity claims"]},
 {c:"Enterprise Value",d:2,q:"Is enterprise value always higher than equity value?",k:["No, only if debt is larger than cash","If cash exceeds debt, EV is lower than equity value","EV = equity + debt + preferred + NCI − cash","Cash-rich tech companies often show this"]},
 {c:"Enterprise Value",d:2,q:"A company's stock price rises 10%. What happens to enterprise value?",k:["Equity value rises 10%","Debt and cash stay the same","So EV rises by the dollar increase in equity value","EV rises by less than 10% if the company has net debt"]},
 {c:"Enterprise Value",d:2,q:"What's the difference between EV/EBITDA and EV/EBIT?",k:["EV/EBITDA ignores depreciation and amortization","EV/EBIT accounts for the cost of using up assets","EV/EBIT is better for capital-intensive companies","EV/EBITDA is easier to compare across firms"]},
 {c:"Enterprise Value",d:2,q:"How do you calculate net debt?",k:["Total debt minus cash and equivalents","Some include preferred stock and other debt-like items","Net debt is the bridge between equity value and EV","Negative net debt means net cash"]},
 {c:"Enterprise Value",d:3,q:"How do convertible bonds affect enterprise value?",k:["If in the money, treat as equity and add the new shares to the count","If out of the money, treat as debt","Never count both the shares and the debt","Test against the current share price"]},
 {c:"Enterprise Value",d:3,q:"Can enterprise value be negative? What does it mean?",k:["Yes, if cash is greater than equity value plus debt","Market values the operating business at less than zero","Seen with cash-rich biotechs or heavily distressed names","Often a sign the market expects cash to be burned"]},
 {c:"Enterprise Value",d:3,q:"Why might you include an unfunded pension in enterprise value?",k:["It's a debt-like obligation the company must fund","Ignoring it overstates the value available to shareholders","Use the after-tax underfunded amount","Common in older industrial companies"]},
 {c:"Valuation & DCF",d:1,q:"What's the difference between intrinsic and relative valuation?",k:["Intrinsic (DCF) values the cash flows of the business itself","Relative (comps) values it against similar companies","Intrinsic relies on assumptions you control","Relative reflects what the market is paying today"]},
 {c:"Valuation & DCF",d:1,q:"What is a P/E ratio and what does it tell you?",k:["Share price divided by earnings per share","How much investors pay for $1 of earnings","High P/E often means higher growth expectations","Distorted by debt levels, so compare carefully"]},
 {c:"Valuation & DCF",d:2,q:"Why use the mid-year convention in a DCF?",k:["Cash flows arrive throughout the year, not at year-end","Discount each year's cash flow by half a year less","This raises the DCF value","More realistic for most operating businesses"]},
 {c:"Valuation & DCF",d:2,q:"What happens to a DCF if the tax rate goes up?",k:["Taxes on EBIT go up, so unlevered free cash flow falls","Lower cash flows mean a lower valuation","WACC effect is small: the debt tax shield rises, but not enough to offset","Also lowers terminal value"]},
 {c:"Valuation & DCF",d:2,q:"How do you calculate cost of equity?",k:["CAPM: risk-free rate + beta × equity risk premium","Risk-free rate is usually the 10-year Treasury yield","Beta measures sensitivity to the market","Some add a size or company-specific premium"]},
 {c:"Valuation & DCF",d:2,q:"How do you calculate terminal value with the exit multiple method?",k:["Final-year EBITDA times an exit multiple","Multiple often comes from comparable companies","Discount that value back to today","Check the implied perpetuity growth rate"]},
 {c:"Valuation & DCF",d:2,q:"What's the difference between trading comps and precedent transactions?",k:["Comps use current trading multiples of public peers","Precedents use prices paid in past acquisitions","Precedents include a control premium, so they're usually higher","Comps are better for minority, day-to-day value"]},
 {c:"Valuation & DCF",d:2,q:"What is a sensitivity table in a DCF and what do you usually flex?",k:["Grid showing value under different assumptions","Common pairs: WACC vs terminal growth, WACC vs exit multiple","Shows how fragile the valuation is","Gives a range, not a single answer"]},
 {c:"Valuation & DCF",d:2,q:"How would you value a private company?",k:["Comps and precedents, adjusting for lack of liquidity","DCF using estimated WACC from public peers","Normalize financials for owner expenses","Apply a discount for size and illiquidity"]},
 {c:"Valuation & DCF",d:3,q:"Walk me through unlevering and relevering beta.",k:["Unlever: levered beta / (1 + (1 − tax) × debt/equity)","Do this for each comp to strip out capital structure","Average the unlevered betas","Relever at the target's debt/equity to get its beta"]},
 {c:"Valuation & DCF",d:3,q:"What is the implied perpetuity growth rate and why check it?",k:["Growth rate implied by an exit-multiple terminal value","Back-solve from terminal value, WACC and final-year cash flow","Should be reasonable, usually near long-run GDP/inflation","A high implied rate means the exit multiple is aggressive"]},
 {c:"Valuation & DCF",d:3,q:"How should stock-based compensation be handled in a DCF?",k:["Treat as a real expense and don't add it back","Or add back and include future dilution in the share count","Not doing either overstates value","Big issue for tech companies"]},
 {c:"Valuation & DCF",d:3,q:"How would you value a highly cyclical company?",k:["Normalize earnings over a full cycle","Use mid-cycle margins and multiples","Avoid applying a peak or trough multiple to peak or trough earnings","Consider asset value as a floor"]},
 {c:"Valuation & DCF",d:3,q:"A comp just completed a large acquisition. How do you handle it in your comps?",k:["Its trailing numbers don't match the new capital structure","Use pro forma financials including the acquired business","Or exclude it from the set if you can't adjust","Always flag the adjustment"]},
 {c:"M&A",d:1,q:"What's the difference between a merger and an acquisition?",k:["Acquisition: one company buys another, which is absorbed","Merger: two companies combine, often described as equals","In practice the line is blurry, and legal structure differs","Stock deals can still make one side the clear buyer"]},
 {c:"M&A",d:1,q:"What's the difference between a buy-side and sell-side advisor?",k:["Sell-side helps the company being sold get the best price and terms","Buy-side helps the acquirer find, value and negotiate for a target","Sell-side runs a marketing process","Buy-side focuses on valuation, synergies and financing"]},
 {c:"M&A",d:2,q:"How does a seller think about getting paid in stock versus cash?",k:["Cash is certain, stock carries risk and ties value to the buyer's performance","Stock lets sellers share in synergies and may defer taxes","Seller reviews the buyer's valuation and quality","Collars can protect the seller if the price moves"]},
 {c:"M&A",d:2,q:"What is a break-up fee?",k:["Fee paid if a deal falls through under certain conditions","Compensates the other side for time and cost","Typically a few percent of deal value","Reverse break-up fees are paid by the buyer, for example on financing or antitrust failure"]},
 {c:"M&A",d:2,q:"What is a tender offer?",k:["Offer made directly to shareholders to buy their shares at a set price","Bypasses the target's board","Often used in hostile situations","Usually has a minimum acceptance threshold"]},
 {c:"M&A",d:2,q:"What is a poison pill?",k:["Shareholder rights plan that makes a hostile takeover very costly","Triggered when a bidder crosses an ownership threshold","Lets other holders buy shares at a discount, diluting the bidder","Gives the board time to find alternatives"]},
 {c:"M&A",d:2,q:"What's the difference between an asset purchase and a stock purchase?",k:["Stock purchase: buyer takes the whole company, including its liabilities","Asset purchase: buyer picks assets and liabilities it wants","Asset deals can give a tax basis step-up","Asset deals need more consents and transfers"]},
 {c:"M&A",d:2,q:"What is a control premium?",k:["Extra amount a buyer pays over market price to gain control","Pays for the ability to run the company and capture synergies","Often cited in the range of 20-40%","Why precedents are typically higher than trading comps"]},
 {c:"M&A",d:3,q:"Why might a company do a deal that's dilutive to EPS?",k:["Strategic fit or long-term growth outweighs short-term EPS","Synergies may take time to arrive","Acquiring a faster-growing business can raise value","Market may look past dilution if it believes the story"]},
 {c:"M&A",d:3,q:"Walk me through how debt financing changes the pro forma balance sheet.",k:["Cash and debt both rise by the amount raised","Cash goes to the sellers or the target's shareholders","Goodwill and acquired assets are added","Financing fees are capitalized and amortized"]},
 {c:"M&A",d:3,q:"What is a collar in a stock deal?",k:["Mechanism limiting how much value or exchange ratio can move","Fixed exchange ratio with a price band, or a floating ratio","Protects sellers from a drop in the buyer's stock","Protects buyers from issuing too many shares"]},
 {c:"M&A",d:3,q:"What antitrust issues can derail a deal?",k:["Regulators can block mergers that reduce competition","Horizontal deals between competitors get the most scrutiny","Can require divestitures or remedies","Long reviews create timing risk, so reverse break-up fees are used"]},
 {c:"M&A",d:3,q:"How do you decide how much of a synergy estimate to credit in the price?",k:["Cost synergies are more reliable than revenue synergies","Haircut the estimates to reflect execution risk","Account for the one-time costs to achieve them","Buyer shouldn't pay away all of the synergies"]},
 {c:"LBO & PE",d:1,q:"What's the difference between private equity and venture capital?",k:["PE buys mature companies, often with leverage","VC invests in early-stage companies, with equity only","PE gets control, VC often takes minority stakes","PE returns come from operations and debt paydown, VC from outsized winners"]},
 {c:"LBO & PE",d:1,q:"What does a private equity associate do day to day?",k:["Source and screen deals","Build LBO models and analyze companies","Run due diligence and prepare investment memos","Support portfolio companies after the deal closes"]},
 {c:"LBO & PE",d:2,q:"A fund targets a 20% IRR over five years. What multiple of money is that?",k:["1.2^5 is about 2.5x","So the investment must grow roughly 2.5x","Shorter holding periods need less MOIC for the same IRR","Rule of thumb: 2x in 5 years is about 15% IRR"]},
 {c:"LBO & PE",d:2,q:"What are the main sources and uses of funds in an LBO?",k:["Sources: senior debt, subordinated debt, sponsor equity, rollover","Uses: purchase of equity, refinance old debt, fees and expenses","Sources must equal uses","Sponsor equity is usually the plug"]},
 {c:"LBO & PE",d:2,q:"What is a management rollover?",k:["Management reinvests part of their proceeds into the new company","Aligns management with the sponsor","Reduces the equity the fund needs to write","Common in founder or family-owned deals"]},
 {c:"LBO & PE",d:2,q:"What's the difference between senior debt and mezzanine?",k:["Senior is paid first, cheaper, and secured","Mezzanine sits below, with higher interest","Mezz often has PIK interest or warrants","Mezz fills the gap between senior debt and equity"]},
 {c:"LBO & PE",d:2,q:"Why do PE firms use more debt on stable businesses?",k:["Predictable cash flow supports interest payments","Lower risk of default","More leverage means a smaller equity check and higher IRR","Cyclical businesses can't carry as much debt"]},
 {c:"LBO & PE",d:2,q:"How do PE funds make money: management fees and carried interest?",k:["Management fee is about 2% of committed capital per year","Carry is roughly 20% of profits above a hurdle","Hurdle is often an 8% preferred return","Carry aligns the GP with LPs"]},
 {c:"LBO & PE",d:2,q:"What is a cash sweep?",k:["Excess cash flow is used to repay debt","Reduces interest and speeds up debt paydown","Typically required by credit agreements","Boosts equity value at exit"]},
 {c:"LBO & PE",d:2,q:"If the purchase price goes up and the exit stays the same, what happens to IRR?",k:["IRR goes down","More equity is needed for the same exit value","To compensate, you need higher growth or lower price","Why bidders compute their max price from target returns"]},
 {c:"LBO & PE",d:3,q:"What does 'ability to pay' mean in an LBO analysis?",k:["Maximum price that still hits the target IRR","Solve for entry price given debt, growth and exit","Shows how high a sponsor could bid","Used to size a bid against strategic buyers"]},
 {c:"LBO & PE",d:3,q:"What happens if a company breaches a loan covenant?",k:["Lenders can demand a waiver fee or amend terms","They can raise the interest rate or tighten terms","In worst cases they can accelerate the debt","Why cushions in covenants matter in an LBO"]},
 {c:"LBO & PE",d:3,q:"What are PIK notes and how do they change an LBO?",k:["Interest paid in additional debt, not cash","Preserves cash flow in early years","Debt balance grows over time","Raises risk and cost at exit"]},
 {c:"LBO & PE",d:3,q:"How are fees and expenses treated in sources and uses?",k:["Financing fees are a use of funds, capitalized and amortized","Advisory fees are also a use of funds","Higher fees mean more equity is required","Fees reduce returns, so they matter in the model"]},
 {c:"LBO & PE",d:3,q:"Why do sponsors avoid relying on multiple expansion in their base case?",k:["Exit multiple is outside their control","Usually assume exit multiple equals the entry multiple","Returns should come from growth and debt paydown","Multiple expansion is upside, not a plan"]},
 {c:"Markets & Hedge Funds",d:1,q:"What's the difference between a stock and a bond?",k:["Stock is ownership in a company","Bond is a loan with fixed payments","Stockholders are paid after bondholders","Bonds are usually less volatile"]},
 {c:"Markets & Hedge Funds",d:1,q:"What is the S&P 500?",k:["Index of 500 large US companies","Market-cap weighted","Common benchmark for US stocks","Reflects the largest companies most"]},
 {c:"Markets & Hedge Funds",d:2,q:"What is the Sharpe ratio?",k:["Excess return over the risk-free rate divided by volatility","Measures return per unit of risk","Higher is better","Used to compare managers"]},
 {c:"Markets & Hedge Funds",d:2,q:"What's the difference between long/short and market-neutral?",k:["Long/short holds longs and shorts, often with net long exposure","Market-neutral aims for near-zero net market exposure","Market-neutral tries to profit from stock selection only","Lower beta, lower market-driven returns"]},
 {c:"Markets & Hedge Funds",d:2,q:"What does an inverted yield curve signal?",k:["Short-term rates are higher than long-term rates","Often seen before recessions","Suggests markets expect slowing growth or rate cuts","Not a perfect predictor"]},
 {c:"Markets & Hedge Funds",d:2,q:"What happens to bond prices when yields rise?",k:["Prices fall","Existing bonds with lower coupons become less attractive","Longer bonds move more","Inverse relationship"]},
 {c:"Markets & Hedge Funds",d:2,q:"What are call and put options?",k:["Call: right to buy at a set price","Put: right to sell at a set price","Buyers pay a premium","Use for speculation or hedging"]},
 {c:"Markets & Hedge Funds",d:2,q:"What does '2 and 20' mean?",k:["2% management fee on assets","20% performance fee on profits","Often with a high-water mark","Why hedge fund returns are quoted net of fees"]},
 {c:"Markets & Hedge Funds",d:3,q:"What is duration?",k:["Measure of a bond's sensitivity to interest rate changes","Roughly the % price change for a 1% change in yield","Longer maturities and lower coupons have higher duration","Key tool for managing interest-rate risk"]},
 {c:"Markets & Hedge Funds",d:3,q:"How does a merger arbitrage strategy work?",k:["Buy the target after a deal is announced","Target trades below the offer price because of deal risk","Profit from the spread if the deal closes","In stock deals, may short the acquirer to hedge"]},
 {c:"Markets & Hedge Funds",d:3,q:"What is a covered call?",k:["Own the stock and sell a call option against it","Collect premium income","Upside is capped at the strike price","Best in flat or mildly rising markets"]},
 {c:"Markets & Hedge Funds",d:3,q:"Walk me through a pairs trade.",k:["Long one stock and short a similar one","Bet on relative performance, not market direction","Reduces market and sector risk","Risk: the relationship breaks down"]},
 {c:"Markets & Hedge Funds",d:3,q:"Why might a stock fall after reporting good earnings?",k:["Expectations were even higher","Weak guidance for the next quarter","Quality of earnings or one-time items","'Buy the rumor, sell the news'"]},
 {c:"Markets & Hedge Funds",d:3,q:"What is a short squeeze?",k:["Heavily shorted stock rises","Short sellers buy to cover and limit losses","Their buying pushes the price even higher","Shows the unlimited loss risk of shorting"]},
 {c:"Restructuring",d:1,q:"What does a restructuring advisor do?",k:["Advises distressed companies, creditors or equity holders","Helps with refinancing, out-of-court deals and bankruptcy","Builds cash forecasts and valuations","Negotiates between stakeholders"]},
 {c:"Restructuring",d:1,q:"Who are the main parties in a bankruptcy?",k:["Debtor: the company","Creditors: lenders, bondholders, suppliers","Equity holders","Court and often a creditors' committee"]},
 {c:"Restructuring",d:2,q:"What is DIP financing?",k:["Debtor-in-possession loan made during Chapter 11","Gets super-priority over existing debt","Provides liquidity to keep operating","Lenders gain control over the process"]},
 {c:"Restructuring",d:2,q:"What is the absolute priority rule?",k:["Senior creditors must be paid in full before juniors get anything","Equity gets paid last","Plan must follow the priority of claims","Exceptions require creditor consent"]},
 {c:"Restructuring",d:2,q:"What is a prepackaged bankruptcy?",k:["Plan is negotiated and voted on before filing","Company spends less time in Chapter 11","Lower costs and less disruption","Needs support from key creditors"]},
 {c:"Restructuring",d:2,q:"What is a debt-for-equity swap?",k:["Creditors exchange debt for ownership in the company","Reduces debt and interest burden","Existing shareholders are diluted","Common outcome of a restructuring"]},
 {c:"Restructuring",d:2,q:"What is the automatic stay?",k:["Triggered upon a bankruptcy filing","Stops most collection actions against the debtor","Gives the company breathing room","Secured creditors can ask for relief"]},
 {c:"Restructuring",d:2,q:"What does an official creditors' committee do?",k:["Represents unsecured creditors in Chapter 11","Hires advisors paid by the estate","Negotiates the plan with the debtor","Monitors the debtor's operations"]},
 {c:"Restructuring",d:3,q:"What is the fulcrum security?",k:["Class of debt that is only partly covered by the company's value","Gets paid in whole or part in the new equity","Holders have the biggest say in the outcome","Juniors below it are usually wiped out"]},
 {c:"Restructuring",d:3,q:"What are the voting requirements for a creditor class to accept a plan?",k:["More than half in number of claims voting","At least two-thirds in dollar amount of claims voting","Calculated per class","Classes that are impaired get to vote"]},
 {c:"Restructuring",d:3,q:"What is a cramdown?",k:["Court confirms a plan even if a class of creditors rejects it","At least one impaired class must accept","Plan must be fair and equitable and not discriminate unfairly","Respects the absolute priority rule"]},
 {c:"Restructuring",d:3,q:"When would a company prefer an out-of-court restructuring over Chapter 11?",k:["Cheaper and faster with less stigma","Works if few creditors and key holders agree","Avoids disrupting customers and suppliers","Chapter 11 helps when holdouts block a deal"]},
 {c:"Restructuring",d:3,q:"What is a stalking horse bid?",k:["First bidder with a negotiated offer in a 363 sale","Sets a floor price that others must beat","Gets protections like a break-up fee","Shortens and de-risks the auction"]},
 {c:"Brain Teasers",d:1,q:"What is 18 × 25?",k:["18 × 100 / 4","1800 / 4 = 450","Answer: 450","Multiplying by 25 is dividing by 4 after multiplying by 100"]},
 {c:"Brain Teasers",d:1,q:"What is 7/8 as a percentage?",k:["1/8 is 12.5%","So 7/8 = 100% − 12.5%","Answer: 87.5%","Memorize the eighths"]},
 {c:"Brain Teasers",d:1,q:"What is 20% of 350?",k:["10% of 350 = 35","Double it: 70","Answer: 70","Check: 350 / 5 = 70"]},
 {c:"Brain Teasers",d:2,q:"A stock rises 20%, then falls 20%. Where does it end up?",k:["Start at 100, up 20% = 120","Down 20% of 120 = 24, so 96","Answer: down 4% from the start","Percentages apply to different bases"]},
 {c:"Brain Teasers",d:2,q:"If you invest at 10% compounded annually, about how long to double your money?",k:["Rule of 72: 72 / 10","Answer: about 7.2 years","Actual is about 7.3 years","Works best for rates between 6% and 10%"]},
 {c:"Brain Teasers",d:2,q:"What is 1 + 2 + 3 + ... + 100?",k:["Pair 1 with 100, 2 with 99, and so on","Each pair sums to 101, and there are 50 pairs","Answer: 5,050","Formula: n(n+1)/2"]},
 {c:"Brain Teasers",d:2,q:"There are three switches outside a closed room and one light bulb inside. You can only enter once. How do you find the right switch?",k:["Turn switch 1 on for a few minutes, then off","Turn switch 2 on and walk in","Bulb on = switch 2","Bulb off but warm = switch 1, off and cold = switch 3"]},
 {c:"Brain Teasers",d:2,q:"What is the probability of rolling a sum of 7 with two dice?",k:["There are 36 equally likely outcomes","Six combinations make 7: 1-6, 2-5, 3-4 and reverses","6/36 = 1/6","7 is the most likely sum"]},
 {c:"Brain Teasers",d:3,q:"You have 100 coins on a table, 10 of them heads up, and you can't see them. Split them into two piles with the same number of heads.",k:["Take any 10 coins and make them pile B","Flip every coin in pile B","Say pile B had k heads: pile A then has 10 − k","After flipping pile B has 10 − k heads, so both have the same number"]},
 {c:"Brain Teasers",d:3,q:"Estimate how many taxis there are in New York City.",k:["Population about 8-9 million","Taxi rides per day: perhaps 400K-500K","Each taxi does maybe 25-30 rides per day","Cross-check the total against what you know about the city's licensed fleet"]},
 {c:"Brain Teasers",d:3,q:"You roll two dice and take the higher number. What's the expected value?",k:["Count outcomes where the max is k: (2k − 1) of 36","E = (1×1 + 2×3 + 3×5 + 4×7 + 5×9 + 6×11) / 36","= 161 / 36","Answer: about 4.47"]},
 {c:"Consulting",d:1,q:"What's the difference between consulting and investment banking day to day?",k:["Consulting: solving client problems, with strategy and operations focus","Banking: transactions, models, deals and financing","Consulting is more structured problem-solving and slides","Banking has more modeling and execution"]},
 {c:"Consulting",d:1,q:"What does MECE mean and why does it matter?",k:["Mutually exclusive, collectively exhaustive","Categories don't overlap and cover everything","Keeps your structure clean and complete","Interviewers watch for it in your frameworks"]},
 {c:"Consulting",d:1,q:"Why do interviewers want you to structure before you calculate?",k:["Shows how you think before diving into numbers","Prevents wasted effort in the wrong direction","Helps you cover the whole problem","Makes your communication easy to follow"]},
 {c:"Consulting",d:2,q:"Estimate how many pizzas are sold in the US on Super Bowl Sunday.",k:["Start with US population of about 330 million","Estimate the share who order pizza on that day","Account for group sizes and pies per order","Sanity check against delivery chain volumes"]},
 {c:"Consulting",d:2,q:"A bank's customers are leaving. How would you structure the problem?",k:["Define the churn: who, since when, which products","Internal causes: pricing, service, product, fees","External causes: competitors, rates, fintech","Then size it and prioritize fixes"]},
 {c:"Consulting",d:2,q:"A client's new product launch missed its sales target. Where do you start?",k:["Compare actual vs target by channel, region and customer","Check the market: demand, competition, pricing","Check the execution: marketing, distribution, product","Find the biggest gap and test hypotheses"]},
 {c:"Consulting",d:2,q:"Price is $50, variable cost is $30 and fixed cost is $400K. How many units give a $100K profit?",k:["Contribution per unit = $20","Needed contribution = $400K + $100K = $500K","$500K / $20 = 25,000 units","Check the answer by recomputing profit"]},
 {c:"Consulting",d:2,q:"A retailer wants to open an online store. How would you evaluate it?",k:["Market: size, growth, customers online","Economics: revenue, margin, shipping, tech costs","Capabilities: logistics, brand, competition","Risks and cannibalization of stores"]},
 {c:"Consulting",d:2,q:"Estimate the number of dentists in the US.",k:["Population around 330 million","One dentist can serve roughly 1,500-2,000 patients","So about 165K-220K dentists","Cross-check against visits per dentist"]},
 {c:"Consulting",d:2,q:"Tell me about a time you led without formal authority.",k:["Set up the goal and why you weren't in charge","Describe how you built buy-in","Show how you handled pushback","State the result"]},
 {c:"Consulting",d:3,q:"A client's merger integration is behind schedule. What do you do?",k:["Identify what is behind: IT, people, process, culture","Prioritize what drives the value of the deal","Set owners, milestones and weekly tracking","Communicate to employees and customers"]},
 {c:"Consulting",d:3,q:"A client wants to enter India. How do you structure the decision?",k:["Market attractiveness: size, growth, regulation","Ability to win: product fit, competitors, capabilities","Entry mode: export, partner, acquire, build","Economics, risks and timeline"]},
 {c:"Consulting",d:3,q:"Your data contradicts your hypothesis. What do you do?",k:["Check the data first for errors","Be willing to drop or revise the hypothesis","Explain what the data does show","Adjust your analysis and next steps"]},
 {c:"Consulting",d:3,q:"A telecom wants to raise its subscription price by 5%. How would you evaluate it?",k:["Estimate price elasticity: how many customers would leave","Compare extra revenue from the rest against lost customers","Look at competitor pricing and switching costs","Consider segments, brand impact and testing the change"]},
 {c:"Consulting",d:3,q:"The CEO insists on a strategy you think is wrong. What do you do?",k:["Make sure you understand the reasoning first","Bring evidence in a respectful, private conversation","Offer a way to test the idea or reduce the risk","If overruled, support the decision and monitor it"]},
 {c:"M&A",d:2,f:FIRM_PACKS[0],q:"A client wants to buy a competitor. How would you run the buy-side process?",k:["Clarify the strategic goal and screen possible targets","Value the best targets: comps, precedents, DCF with synergies","Plan the approach, confidentiality and timing","Support diligence, financing and negotiation of terms"]},
 {c:"M&A",d:2,f:FIRM_PACKS[0],q:"A bidder offers stock instead of cash. How does that change your analysis?",k:["Value depends on the buyer's share price, so assess its quality","Check the exchange ratio and any collar","Think about taxes, and whether the seller shares in synergies","Compare with a cash bid on risk-adjusted value"]},
 {c:"M&A",d:3,f:FIRM_PACKS[0],q:"What is a go-shop provision and why would a board agree to one?",k:["Period after signing when the target can solicit better offers","Often used when a board did not run a full auction","Supports the board's duty to get the best price","Usually comes with a lower break-up fee during the window"]},
 {c:"M&A",d:3,f:FIRM_PACKS[0],q:"Why would a board form a special committee?",k:["Handles conflicts of interest, for example a management buyout","Made up of independent directors with their own advisors","Negotiates on behalf of minority shareholders","Helps protect the board in later legal challenges"]},
 {c:"M&A",d:3,f:FIRM_PACKS[1],q:"A company is deciding between a spin-off and a sale of a division. How do you compare them?",k:["Sale: cash now, a clean exit, tax leakage possible","Spin-off: shareholders keep exposure, tax-free if structured properly","Compare value to shareholders in each case, and stranded costs","Consider the buyer's appetite, timing and market conditions"]},
 {c:"M&A",d:3,f:FIRM_PACKS[1],q:"Besides price, what does a board weigh when comparing bids?",k:["Certainty to close: financing, regulatory and diligence risk","Form of consideration: cash, stock or mix","Timing and conditions","Employees, strategy and reputational factors"]},
 {c:"M&A",d:3,f:FIRM_PACKS[1],q:"A stock is down 30% in a month and an activist appears. How do you advise the company?",k:["Understand what the activist wants and what's driving the drop","Assess the company's standalone plan and valuation gap","Prepare a response: engage, make changes or defend","Consider defenses and shareholder communications"]},
 {c:"M&A",d:3,f:FIRM_PACKS[1],q:"How would you evaluate a stock-for-stock merger of equals?",k:["Contribution analysis: each side's share of revenue, EBITDA, equity value","Exchange ratio and ownership split","Synergies and who captures them","Governance: board, CEO, headquarters"]},
 {c:"LBO & PE",d:2,f:FIRM_PACKS[2],q:"How would you diligence customer concentration risk?",k:["Look at revenue by customer and top-10 share","Check contract terms and renewal history","Talk to key customers","Model downside if the largest customer leaves"]},
 {c:"LBO & PE",d:2,f:FIRM_PACKS[2],q:"What's the difference between a platform and an add-on acquisition?",k:["Platform: the first company in a new strategy with management and scale","Add-ons: smaller acquisitions bolted on to the platform","Add-ons are often bought at lower multiples","Buy-and-build can create multiple arbitrage"]},
 {c:"LBO & PE",d:3,f:FIRM_PACKS[2],q:"How do you build and think about the downside case in a deal?",k:["Stress revenue, margins and exit multiple","Test leverage and covenant headroom","Ask what has to be true for you to lose money","Decide whether the risk is worth the base-case return"]},
 {c:"LBO & PE",d:3,f:FIRM_PACKS[2],q:"Why might a seller pick a lower bid over the highest price?",k:["Greater certainty of closing","Fewer conditions or financing risk","Better fit with management or employees","Faster timeline or all-cash terms"]},
 {c:"Restructuring",d:2,f:FIRM_PACKS[3],q:"What does a 13-week cash flow forecast tell you?",k:["Weekly view of cash receipts and disbursements","Shows how much runway a company has","Used to size DIP or liquidity needs","Key tool for lenders and advisors in distress"]},
 {c:"Restructuring",d:2,f:FIRM_PACKS[3],q:"How does a distressed exchange differ from a bankruptcy filing?",k:["Out-of-court deal where creditors swap old debt for new terms","Needs creditor consent and avoids court costs","Holdouts can block it","Still counted as a default by rating agencies"]},
 {c:"Restructuring",d:3,f:FIRM_PACKS[3],q:"Lenders want to take control through a debt-for-equity swap. What would existing equity argue?",k:["Value is higher than lenders claim, so they deserve recovery","Operating turnaround or market recovery can restore value","Challenge the valuation and the plan terms","Seek rights or an option to keep some ownership"]},
 {c:"Restructuring",d:3,f:FIRM_PACKS[3],q:"A company has two competing lender groups. How do you think about it as the company's advisor?",k:["Figure out what each group wants and their leverage","Look for a path that keeps the business operating","Run valuation scenarios to see who is the fulcrum","Aim for a deal everyone prefers to a messy filing"]},
 {c:"Markets & Hedge Funds",d:2,f:FIRM_PACKS[4],q:"How do you think about position sizing?",k:["Size by conviction and by risk","Limit loss on any single position","Consider liquidity and correlation","Bigger bets need stronger evidence"]},
 {c:"Markets & Hedge Funds",d:3,f:FIRM_PACKS[4],q:"Rates rise 100 bps unexpectedly. Which sectors do you worry about?",k:["Highly levered companies with floating-rate debt","Long-duration growth stocks, whose cash flows are further out","Real estate and utilities, often valued on yield","Banks could benefit from wider margins if funding costs stay contained"]},
 {c:"Markets & Hedge Funds",d:3,f:FIRM_PACKS[4],q:"A position moves against you by 15%. What do you do?",k:["Re-check the thesis: has anything changed?","Separate noise from new information","Cut if the thesis is broken, hold or add if intact and risk allows","Follow your risk limits regardless"]},
 {c:"Markets & Hedge Funds",d:3,f:FIRM_PACKS[4],q:"How do you tell a cheap stock from a value trap?",k:["Cheap for a reason: shrinking revenue or margins","Look for a catalyst that can close the gap","Check balance sheet strength","Compare to history and peers, adjusting for quality"]}
